Unreleased
    Allow a published clean replay to supersede an installed repair whose
    registration failed, retaining the original receipt and queued intent.
    Keep agent notes and protocol files local instead of adding them to
    source patches. Isolate fixture repair locks from live installer runs.
    Inventory native-source distributions against the installed interpreter
    build and current patch digests with planning/rc-xs-coverage. Accept
    author/distribution review queues through install-ported --from FILE.
    Publication overlays only the registry checkout's own changes since its
    merge base with upstream. A checkout older than upstream no longer
    deletes or replaces newer published data; a status record changed on
    both sides is merged and any other file changed on both sides stops
    publication with the file named.
    Ask repair agents to check upstream repositories, issues and pending
    fixes before editing. Supply release metadata resources and retain
    research results in receipts, with public links in registry evidence.
    Publish each completed distribution automatically with a Git ports
    registry, including no-change successes and unresolved failures.
    --publish auto|direct|pr|off selects direct pushes or batched fork PRs.
    Keep a separate publication checkout and durable retry queue, preserve
    unrelated work, and merge concurrent status observations. Publication
    failures leave build results intact and resume without rebuilding.
    Add stop --workdir DIR to drain active builds and repair workers without
    starting queued work. Save stopped results and continue with --resume.
    Provide built-in Codex and Claude Code repair adapters and shared repair
    instructions. --agent codex and --agent claude need no wrapper script.
    TOML profiles select models, effort and executables. Claude repairs
    default to the sonnet alias rather than the CLI's own default model.
    Saved sessions keep the provider configuration and allow an explicit
    selection on resume.
    Register successful AI repairs automatically in a writable ports
    registry. Keep exact release selectors, patch digests, recipe links,
    and portable evidence. Exact-release validation now accepts distributions
    that install tools or C headers without a Perl module.
    Allow repair agents to work on the defective layer and return a clear
    human handoff when progress stops. Keep the ten-minute call timeout as
    a hang guard and allow sixteen repair attempts per release by default.
    Rename the command to xperl-install; the XPerl interpreter keeps the
    xperl name. Update resume instructions and bootstrap examples.
    Add scan-impact to find candidate CPAN source affected by core changes.
    Load an installed module in a child interpreter when static metadata
    cannot evaluate its version. This lets Thrift's modules use their shared
    runtime version for both versioned and unversioned prerequisites.
    Repair agents can return an undeclared CPAN prerequisite for the scheduler
    to install before retrying the release. Their response file now lives in
    the source directory.
    Repair agents can report missing host dependencies and other user actions
    through a JSON response. Receipts and batch results preserve the action,
    and repair stops without repeated source-free calls. Every install saves
    a session for --resume; --action-shell opens an interactive shell once
    for queued actions and retries after it exits.
    Exclude generated objects and test scratch files from AI patch proposals,
    so an agent that rebuilds a distribution cannot make proposal generation
    fail on binary or unrecognized temporary files.
    AI repairs allow sixteen attempts per release by default and start at least
    ten seconds apart across installer processes. --repair-limit is an
    optional run-wide cap, not a default that stops the batch after one
    release. --repair-interval controls the spacing.
    Stop repair attempts when an agent leaves the source unchanged. Receipts
    mark the unchanged attempt so missing external prerequisites do not spend
    the remaining repair budget on identical retries.
    install --prefix set to the interpreter's own prefix installs into its
    site library, which @INC already searches, instead of PREFIX/lib/perl5.
    --layout auto|site|install_base (or install.layout) chooses explicitly;
    other prefixes keep the INSTALL_BASE layout. Receipts record the layout
    and any older copy under PREFIX/lib/perl5 that would hide the new one.
    Failed releases enter a repair queue while independent builds continue.
    --repair-jobs controls repair workers separately from --jobs; batches
    default to four build workers and one repair worker.
    Builds find C libraries installed by Homebrew: its include, lib and
    lib/pkgconfig directories are added to CPATH, LIBRARY_PATH and
    PKG_CONFIG_PATH. --native-prefix DIR (or install.native_prefixes)
    chooses other prefixes, --no-native-prefix none; receipts record them.
    Releases whose META.yml holds JSON, or whose metadata names the main
    module instead of the distribution, install instead of failing.
    Configure, build, test and install phases time out after --phase-timeout
    seconds (default 3600), stopping every process the phase started.
    The CPAN package index is parsed once per run instead of once per
    lookup, which makes resolving many modules much faster.
    install accepts several modules or --from FILE and keeps going past
    failures, reporting the outcome of each requested module.
    install-ported accepts --jobs K and builds the selected releases with
    the parallel scheduler; progress lines mark resumed entries.
    Repair calls can be capped per run with --repair-limit when requested.
    install accepts --jobs K (or install.jobs in the config) to build
    independent releases in parallel. Receipts record phase start and finish
    times.
    Rebuild from clean sources after an external repair, so make programs
    with one-second timestamps cannot test stale build products.
    Tests compare canonical paths and pass on macOS.
    Receipts record the platform: operating system, architecture name,
    compiler, make program, thread support, and XPerl commit.
    Add install-ported, which rebuilds every release recorded as successful
    in the ports registry with a dry run, filters, resumable state, and
    JSON and text reports.

